<start_of_turn>model
","a) **Serial System**:  
   - A series circuit is a series combination of resistors (or in general, components). The system fails if one or more components fail.  
   - The reliability function \( R_S(t) \) for a series system is the product of the reliability functions of each component.  
   - If the system consists of \( n \) independent components with reliability functions \( R_1(t), R_2(t), R_3(t); \), \( R_n(t) \), the reliability function is:  
     \[
     R_S(t) = R_1(t) \times R_2(t) \times R_3(t) \times R_4(t) \times R_n(t) = \sum_{_{R_i(t)}} R_1(t) \times R_2(t) \times R_3(t) \times R_4(t) \times R_n(t)
     \]
   - The failure factor \( f_S(t) \) is:  
     \[
     f_S(t) = 1 - R_S(t) = 1 - \left(R_1(t) \times R_2(t) \times R_3(t) \times R_4(t) \times R_n(t)\right)
     \]

b) **Parallel System**:  
   - A parallel circuit is a parallel combination of resistors (or in general, components). The system remains operational if one or more components still operational.  
   - The reliability function \( R_S(t) \) for a parallel system is the complement of the failure factor, which is the sum of the probabilities of all components failing.  
   - If the system consists of \( n \) independent components with reliability functions \( R_1(t), R_2(t), R_3(t); \), \( R_n(t) \), the reliability function is:  
     \[
     R_S(t) = 1 - \left(1 - R_1(t)\right) \times (1 - R_2(t)) \times (1 - R_3(t)) \times (1 - R_4(t}) \times (1 - R_n(t))
     \]
