Given:
- [Fe$^{3+}$] = 1.0 M
- K$_{eq}$ = 1.2 x 10$^{20}$
- Moles of KCN added = 0.25 moles

First, we need to find the concentration of KCN added. Since we don't have the volume of the solution, we will assume that the volume of the solution is 1 L.

[CN$^-$] = $\frac{0.25 \text{ moles}}{1 \text{ L}} = 0.25 \text{ M}$

Now, we can set up an ICE (Initial, Change, Equilibrium) table to determine the new equilibrium concentrations of Fe$^{3+}$, CN$^-$, and Fe(CN)}_6^{4-}$.

| Fe$^{3+}$ | CN$^-$ | Fe(CN)}_6^{4-} |
|----------------|----------------|----------------|
| 1.0 M | 0.1 M (initial) | 0 M (initial) |
| -x | -6x | +x |
| 1.0 - x | 0.1 - 6x | x |

The initial concentration of CN$^-$ is 0.1 M because the initial concentration of Fe$^{3+}$ is 1.0 M, and each Fe$^{3+}$ ion requires 6 CN$^-$ ions to form the complex ion.

