Passing \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) gas into a mixture of \( \text{Mn}^{2+}, \text{Ni}^{2+}, \text{Cu}^{2+}, \) and \( \text{Hg}^{2+} \) ions in an acidified aqueous solution precipitates which sulfides?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The sulfide ion (\( \text{S}^{2-} \)) reacts with metal ions to form sulfides. The solubility of these sulfides depends on the solubility product (\( K_{sp} \)) of the sulfide salt. Generally, sulfides of heavier metals are less soluble and precipitate more readily. Here's the order of decreasing basicity of the sulfides, which corresponds to the order of solubility order.

1. **HgS (Lead sulfide)**: The sulfide of mercury (\( \text{HgS} \)) is highly insoluble and precipitates under all conditions.
   \[
   \text{Hg}^{2+} + \text{S}^{2-} \rightarrow \text{HgS} \downarrow
   \]

2. **CuS (Copper sulfide)**: The sulfide of copper (\( \text{CuS} \)) is also insoluble and precipitates under acidic conditions.
   \[
   \text{Cu}^{2+} + \text{S}^{2-} \rightarrow \text{CuS} \downarrow
   \]

3. **NiS (Nickel sulfide)**: The sulphide of nickel (\( \text{NiS} \)) is slightly less insoluble than CuS and HgS, but it precipitates in acidic conditions.
   \[
   \text{Ni}^{2+} + \text{S}^{2-} \rightarrow \text{NiS} \downarrow
   \]

4. **MnS (Manganese sulfide)**: The sulphide of manganese (\( \text{MnS} \)) is the least insoluble sulfide in this case and precipitates under acidic conditions.
   \[
   \text{Mn}^{2+} + \text{S^{2-}} \rightarrow \text{MnS} \downarrow
   \]

Thus, all sulfides (\( \text{HgS} \), \( \text{CuS} \), \( \text{NiS} \), and \( \text{MnS} \)) will precipitate when \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) gas is passed into the mixture of \( \text{Mn}^{2+}, \text{Ni}^{2+}, \text{Cu}^{2+}, \) and \( \text{Hg}^{2+} \) ions in an acidified aqueous solution.",ground_truth_similarity,"# Task Description
