Let's denote the distance \( d \) from the starting point as a function of time \( t \). For \( a \), the distance is given by:
\[
d_a = 10t
\]
For \( b \), the starting distance is 0, but \( b \) starts after 3 hours. Let \( t' \) be the time from when \( b \) starts. Then \( t' = t - 3 \). The distance \( d \) from the starting point for \( b \) is:
\[
d_b = 20(t - 3)
\]
When \( b \) contacts \( a \), they are at the same distance:
\[
d_a = d_b
\]
\[
10t = 20(t - 3)
\]
\[
10t = 20t - 60
\]
\[
t = 6 \text{ hours}
\]
Thus, \( b \) contacts \( a \) after 6 hours from the start of \( a \). The distance from the starting point is:
\[
d = 10 \times 6 = 60 \text{ km}
\]
Alternatively, the distance \( d \) from the starting point for \( b \) is:
\[
d = 20(6 - 3) = 20 \times 3 = 60 \text{ km}
\]
