A proton with mass \(1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}\) and charge \(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\) accelerates from rest in a uniform electric field of strength \(500 \, \text{N/C}\).  
(a) What is the magnitude of the acceleration of the proton?  
(b) How long does it take the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\)?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
(a) The acceleration of the proton is given by Newton's second law:  
\[
F = ma
\]  
The force on the proton is due to the electric field:  
\[
F = qE
\]  
Substituting \(f = qE\) into Newton's second law:  
\[
qE = ma
\]  
Solving for acceleration:  
\[
a = \frac{qE}{m} = \frac{(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C})}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.81 \times 10^{8} \, \text{m/s}^2
\]  
The magnitude of the acceleration is \(\boxed{4.81 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}^2}\).

(b) The acceleration is constant, so the velocity of the proton is:  
\[
v = at
\]  
Solving for time:  
\[
t = \frac{v}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s}}{4.81 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}^2} = 7.28 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{s}
\]  
Thus, the time it take for the proton to reach a speed of \(\boxed{7.28 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{s}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

