</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The decay length (\(\lambda_c\)) of an evanescent wave is given by:  
\[
\lambda_c = \frac{2\pi}{k_f}
\]  
where \(k_f\) is the free-order propagation angle.  

The far-order coupling constant (\(k_f\)) is related to the difference between the evanescent mode's evanescent wave propagation angle (\(k_e\)) and the cladding's propagation angle (\(k_c\)):  
\[
k_f = k_e - k_c
\]  

The group indices (\(n_e\) and \(n_c\)) are related to the group numbers (\(n_f\) and \(n_c\)):  
\[
k_e = 2\pi n_e \lambda^{-1}, \quad k_c = 2\pi n_c \lambda^{-1}
\]  

Thus,  
\[
k_f = 2\pi \left(n_e - n_c\right) \lambda^{-1}
\]  

Substituting this into the decay length formula:  
\[
\lambda_c = \frac{2\pi}{2\pi \left(n_e - n_c\right) \lambda^{-1}} = \frac{1}{(n_e - n_c)} \lambda
\]  

Given \(n_e - n_c \sim 0.3\) and \(\lambda = 1 \mu m = 1 \times 10^{-6} \text{ m}\), the decay length is:  
\[
\lambda_c = \frac{1}{0.3} \times 10^{-6} \text{ m} \approx 3.33 \times 10^{-6} \text{ m}
\]  

Thus, the decay length is \(\boxed{3.33 \times 10^{-6} \text{ m}}\) or \(\boxed{3.33 \mu m}\).
