2. **Calculate the new current after the gain decreases**:
   \[
   A_w = \frac{V_{out}}{V_{in}} = \frac{100}{10} = 10 \)
   \[
   A_w = 100 \times 10^{-6} = 1000
   \]
   Substituting \( V_{in} = 1 \) V, \( V_{out} = 10 \) V.

3. **Calculate the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \) with the initial \( A_w \)**:
   \[
   A_{vf} = \frac{A_w}{1 + m_v A_w} = \frac{100}{1 + 0.1 \times 100} = \frac{100}{11} \approx 9.09 \text{ V/V}
   \]

4. **Calculate the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \) with the new \( A_w \)**:
   \[
   A_{ff} = \frac{A_w}{1 + m_w A_w} = \frac{100}{1 + 0.1 \times 10} = \frac{100}{11} \approx 9.09 \text{ V/V}
   \]

5. **Calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain**:
   \[
   \text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{A_{ff} - A_{ff}}{A_{ff}} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{9.09 - 9.09}{9.09} \right) \times 100 = 0 \times 100 = 0\%
   \]

**Final Answer:**
The percentage change in the closed-loop gain is \(\boxed{0\%}\).
