","Along the length of a constant-area fin, the temperature gradient decreases. This is because the fin dissipates heat to the surroundings, causing the temperature to decrease as the fin length increases. The rate of temperature change (temperature gradient) is given by the heat flux divided by the thermal conductivity of the fin material. Since the heat flux decreases with length, the temperature gradient also decreases. 

Mathematically, the temperature gradient is given by \( \frac{dT}{dx} \), where \( T \) is the temperature and \( x \) is the distance along the fin length. For a heat exchanger fin, the heat flux decreases with distance, leading to a decrease in \( \frac{dT}{dx} \). 

